\subsection{Correction Guidance}
\label{sec:correction}

We evaluate five correction methods across all seven topologies under six error structures (210 configurations total): naive plug-in, maximum likelihood adjustment (MLA), design-based supervised learning (DSL)~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023}, MC-SIMEX, and prediction-powered inference (PPI++)~\cite{angelopoulos2023}. Full per-topology results are in Table~\ref{tab:correction} and the decision procedure in Algorithm~\ref{alg:correction} (Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}).

\paragraph{FWL invariance.}
For four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias), the Frisch--Waugh--Lovell theorem implies that applying $C^{-1}$ to the dummy variables cannot change the $T$ coefficient. Naive plug-in and MLA achieve exactly 0.0\% bias reduction across these four topologies.

\paragraph{Method ranking.}
PPI++ achieves the highest grand-mean bias reduction (93.8\%) with nominal coverage (96.8\%), but requires gold-standard labels. MC-SIMEX provides moderate gold-free correction (20.9\%), serving as the only viable fallback for IV (64.7\% reduction). DSL corrects confounding (49.8\%) and mediation (70.3\%) but catastrophically amplifies bias for M-bias ($-$768\%). However, MC-SIMEX is harmful for front-door ($-$55.7\%) and exposure ($-$3.8\%), and should only be applied after topology identification. No single method dominates: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Measurement error in causal inference.}
The statistical foundations of measurement error are established in \citet{carroll2006} and \citet{gustafson2004}, with the starred-node DAG framework for reasoning about measurement bias formalized by \citet{hernan_cole2009}. For binary variables, non-differential misclassification biases estimates toward the null~\cite{brenner1993, ogburn_vanderweele2012}. \citet{dosemeci1990} first demonstrated that this guarantee fails for polytomous exposures, and \citet{jurek2005} clarified that the toward-null intuition is frequently misapplied when $K \geq 3$. \citet{veirod_laake2001} derived bias expressions for multi-category exposure misclassification in Poisson regression, and \citet{edwards_lessler_naimi2021} showed that dependent misclassification can bias in either direction. However, all of these works analyze bias within a single DAG topology. Our taxonomy is the first to systematically characterize how the \emph{same} $K$-class confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across seven canonical topologies.

\paragraph{Text classifiers and LLM annotation in causal studies.}
\citet{grimmer2022} provide the methodological foundation for using text as data in social science, while \citet{wood_doughty2018} first applied DAG reasoning to text classifier measurement error. \citet{teblunthuis2024} provided the most complete binary treatment: four DAG scenarios with testable conditions and a Maximum Likelihood Adjustment method, but restricted to $K{=}2$. \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended to categorical LLM classifiers but covered only unobserved confounding. On the annotation quality side, \citet{baumann2025} demonstrated that 31\% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with LLM annotations, \citet{kim_garg2025} showed 60\% error correlation across models, and \citet{messing2026} decomposed LLM pipeline variance but excluded causal identification. These works either restrict to binary labels, cover a single topology, or diagnose annotation quality without connecting it to causal estimate validity. Our framework provides the missing causal-analytic link from multi-class confusion matrix structure to topology-specific bias, sensitivity, and correction.

\paragraph{Sensitivity analysis and quantitative bias analysis.}
Our ASV framework draws on two traditions. First, \citet{rosenbaum2002} established sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding, and \citet{vanderweele_ding2017} introduced E-values as interpretable scalar measures of the minimum confounding strength needed to explain away an observed association. \citet{ding_vanderweele2016} developed assumption-free sensitivity bounds, and \citet{smith_vanderweele2021} proposed bounds for multiple bias sources. Second, \citet{lash2009} developed probabilistic bias analysis (PBA) using confusion matrices as sensitivity parameters for misclassification. Our ASVs differ from both traditions in several ways: unlike the scalar E-value, ASVs comprise three complementary types (magnitude, significance, and sign-flip), because annotation error failure modes are topology-dependent; unlike PBA's simulation-based approach for binary settings, ASVs provide formal monotonicity and sharpness guarantees over $K{\times}K$ confusion matrices; and ASVs yield a single scalar report ($\delta^*$) per topology, facilitating communication alongside standard causal estimates.

\paragraph{Correction methods.}
Prediction-powered inference (PPI++)~\cite{angelopoulos2023} and design-based supervised learning (DSL)~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023, gligoric2025} provide general frameworks for valid inference with ML-generated labels. \citet{zrnic_candes2024} extended PPI via cross-prediction, and \citet{audinet2025} benchmarked these methods across datasets. These tools are designed as topology-agnostic. Our contribution reveals that this agnosticism is costly: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent, with matrix-based methods provably ineffective for four of seven topologies. Our ASV analysis identifies \emph{when} correction is needed and \emph{which} failure mode to address, while PPI/DSL provide the \emph{how}.

\begin{abstract}
Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as annotators in computational social science, yet annotation errors propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that binary measurement error theory cannot predict. We present the first systematic framework characterizing how $K$-class confusion matrix structure interacts with DAG topology to determine bias direction and magnitude. We derive bias expressions for seven canonical DAG topologies under multi-class non-differential misclassification (closed-form for confounding, exposure, and mediation; numerically validated for all seven) and establish a structural sign-flip guarantee (with formal proofs for three topologies and Monte Carlo validation for all seven): five topologies (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias, front-door) never produce sign reversal regardless of the confusion matrix, while exposure and instrumental variable topologies can. In the exposure DAG, 83.4\% of $K{=}3$ confusion matrices cause sign reversal, a failure that persists at 13.7\% violation rate (2.1\% sign reversal, 11.7\% amplification) even with classifier accuracy above 70\%. We introduce Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs), a sensitivity analysis framework with three complementary measures that reveal a topology-dependent vulnerability spectrum: IV estimates are maximally vulnerable (52.6\% sign reversal; sign-flip ASV $= 1.1$--$6.7$) while M-bias adjustment is immune (ASV $= \infty$). We evaluate five correction methods across all topologies and prove a Frisch--Waugh--Lovell invariance result: matrix-based corrections achieve 0\% bias reduction on the target coefficient for four topologies, while PPI++ achieves 93.8\% reduction but requires gold labels. Empirical validation on three CSS tasks ($K{=}2,3,10$) with four LLMs confirms the theoretical predictions: IV bias ranges from 30\% at $K{=}10$ to 252\% at $K{=}3$ across annotation settings, and zero sign flips occur across all 98 scenarios. Neither classification $F_1$ nor correction method choice alone determines downstream safety; topology-aware analysis is necessary for valid causal inference with LLM-generated annotations.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large language models are rapidly replacing human annotators in computational social science, with researchers using LLM-generated labels for tasks ranging from sentiment classification to political stance detection~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt, ziems2024can, egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023, baumann2025}. Crucially, most of these tasks are inherently multi-class: stance detection uses 3--4 categories, sentiment analysis 5, and disaster response taxonomies 10 or more. This shift promises dramatic cost reduction, but introduces a critical validity gap: annotation errors from imperfect classifiers propagate into downstream causal estimates in ways that depend on both the error structure and the causal design. \citet{baumann2025} demonstrate that across 37 annotation tasks, 31\% of hypotheses yield incorrect conclusions with state-of-the-art LLMs, rising to 50\% for smaller models; these errors are highly correlated across models (60\% agreement when both err)~\cite{kim_garg2025}, undermining ensemble-based mitigation. Analyzing the \emph{structure} of annotation error, rather than simply reducing its magnitude, is essential for valid downstream inference.

Even under realistic classifier accuracy (diagonal $\geq 0.7$), 13.7\% of $K{=}3$ confusion matrices violate the classical binary guarantee, with 2.1\% causing sign reversal of the estimated treatment effect. Across the full Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ parameter space, sign reversal reaches 83.4\%. This breaks the guarantee that non-differential misclassification biases estimates ``toward the null''~\cite{brenner1993}, a guarantee that many applied researchers implicitly rely upon. Standard classification metrics do not predict this risk: within a narrow $F_1$ range of 0.614--0.690 across three frontier LLMs, relative bias varies by an order of magnitude across topologies (from 4.1\% to 42.1\%), illustrating that \emph{topology}, not annotator quality, determines downstream risk.

The mechanism is algebraic, and the transition at $K {\geq} 3$ is a qualitative break, not a gradual degradation. For $K{=}2$, the stochastic constraint forces any confusion matrix with $C_{kk} {>} 0.5$ to preserve coefficient signs---an algebraic property of the $2{\times}2$ matrix dimension, not of a specific error rate. This guarantee \emph{vanishes} at $K {\geq} 3$: the higher-dimensional confusion matrix admits negative effective weights on categories with opposite-sign coefficients, enabling sign reversal even when every diagonal entry exceeds $0.5$~\cite{veirod_laake2001}. This fundamental asymmetry has received limited systematic treatment. \citet{brenner1993} identified the possibility of polytomous reversal but did not characterize it across causal designs. \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended classifier error analysis to categorical variables and LLM classifiers, but covered only one DAG topology (unobserved confounding). The possibility of polytomous sign reversal was noted by \citet{dosemeci1990} and \citet{veirod_laake2001}; our contribution extends this to a systematic characterization across seven DAG topologies and provides the ASV framework for quantifying vulnerability.

We address these gaps with three contributions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Multi-class bias taxonomy.} We derive bias expressions for seven canonical DAG topologies (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_taxonomy}) under $K$-class non-differential misclassification, prove sign preservation for confounding and collider, verify it numerically for mediation, M-bias, and front-door, and show that exposure and IV can produce sign reversal. Binarization amplifies the gap: collapsing $K{=}3$ to binary introduces additional bias varying 154$\times$ across topologies.

\item \textbf{Annotation Sensitivity Values (ASVs).} We propose three complementary sensitivity measures---magnitude, significance, and sign-flip ASV---revealing a vulnerability spectrum from IV (maximally fragile, 52.6\% sign reversal) to M-bias (immune, ASV $= \infty$).

\item \textbf{Topology-aware correction guidance.} We evaluate five correction methods and show that matrix-based corrections achieve exactly 0\% bias reduction for four topologies due to Frisch--Waugh--Lovell invariance. PPI++ achieves 47.0\% RMSE reduction but requires gold labels and is harmful for M-bias ($-$88\%).
\end{enumerate}

